"""Course-specific glossary source. Run before build.py after changing entries."""
import json
from pathlib import Path

ENTRIES = [
 ('host','Host','A computer participating in a network. A host can run many processes and have several network interfaces.'),
 ('process','Process','A running program. A server process must listen on an address and port to accept network connections.'),
 ('client','Client','In a request/response exchange, the program that requests work and receives the response. Both client and server send and receive data; the same program can be a client on one connection and a server on another.'),
 ('server','Server','In a request/response exchange, the program that accepts a request and sends a response. Sending that response does not make it a client. The word can also refer to a machine or a whole service; check the context.'),
 ('eval-harness','Eval harness','Software that organizes evaluation tests: loading cases, configuring runs, assigning work, collecting results, and arranging scoring. It may run in one process or use many workers; it differs from the agent harness that manages an agent’s action loop.'),
 ('eval-worker','Eval worker','The part of an eval harness that executes assigned test cases and records outcomes. In this course’s example it is a Python process making model API calls. For agent tests it may run or control the agent being evaluated.'),
 ('agent','Agent','In this course, a model-driven system that can choose actions, use tools, and use their results to continue a task. In an agent evaluation it is the system being tested, while an eval worker runs or coordinates the test.'),
 ('agent-harness','Agent harness','Code around a model that manages an agent’s action loop, tool execution, and task state. An eval harness instead organizes and measures tests; an eval worker may run an agent harness locally or control one elsewhere.'),
 ('model-gateway','Model gateway','An intermediary service that accepts model API requests and forwards them to a model-serving backend. It may check API keys, enforce rate limits, or select a backend. It serves the caller and acts as a client of the backend; it is optional in the request path.'),
 ('inference-backend','Inference backend','A service that runs a trained model to generate output. In this course’s example it receives requests from a model gateway and returns output through it. A worker can also call it directly; inference service and model server name the same role here.'),
 ('packet','Packet','A bounded unit of data carried by a network protocol, such as an IP packet. An application message can span many packets.'),
 ('frame','Frame','A unit sent over a link-layer network, such as Ethernet. A frame can carry an IP packet as its payload.'),
 ('protocol','Protocol','An agreement about message formats and behavior between communicating participants, including what they send and how they respond.'),
 ('encapsulation','Encapsulation','Wrapping higher-level data in a lower-level protocol’s headers or other framing. Receivers interpret the layers in reverse order.'),
 ('ip-address','IP address','An Internet Protocol address used for delivery in a particular network context. An address does not by itself identify a person or grant permission.'),
 ('port','Port','A transport-level number that helps distinguish communication endpoints on a host. TCP port 443 and UDP port 443 are different protocol endpoints.'),
 ('socket','Socket','An operating-system communication endpoint used by a process. Network sockets are associated with protocols and addresses; not every socket uses IP.'),
 ('loopback','Loopback','An interface for communication within the local network environment. 127.0.0.1 is an IPv4 loopback address; ::1 is its familiar IPv6 counterpart.'),
 ('localhost','Localhost','A conventional hostname for loopback. Inside a container or Pod, its meaning follows that environment’s network namespace.'),
 ('listener','Listener','A server socket waiting for incoming connections on a bound address and port. A running process may have no listener, or may listen only on loopback.'),
 ('dns','DNS','The Domain Name System: a distributed system for looking up records associated with names. Resolving an address does not prove the application is reachable.'),
 ('resolver','Resolver','Software that performs name lookups. A recursive DNS resolver can obtain answers from other DNS servers and cache them.'),
 ('ttl','TTL','Time to live. In DNS, a cache lifetime for a record. In IP discussions, TTL refers to a different field limiting packet lifetime through hops; do not confuse the two.'),
 ('cname','CNAME','A DNS record that makes one name an alias for another canonical name. Resolving the alias can require additional lookups.'),
 ('nxdomain','NXDOMAIN','A DNS response indicating that the queried name does not exist according to that response. It is distinct from a resolver failing to answer.'),
 ('cidr','CIDR','Classless Inter-Domain Routing notation represents an address prefix using an address and slash length. In IPv4, /24 leaves eight bits for addresses in the range.'),
 ('subnet','Subnet','An address range and associated network subdivision. Provider rules determine which addresses can be assigned to resources.'),
 ('route','Route','A forwarding rule connecting a destination prefix to a next hop or interface. A route alone does not prove permissions or a working return path.'),
 ('router','Router','A device or system forwarding traffic between IP networks. Forwarding commonly uses the most specific matching destination prefix.'),
 ('switch','Switch','A device commonly used to forward frames within a link-layer network. Modern hardware may also route IP traffic, so the exact role matters.'),
 ('gateway','Gateway','A system connecting or mediating between environments. A default IP gateway forwards packets; an API gateway applies application-level policy. Ask which meaning is intended.'),
 ('nat','NAT','Network Address Translation: rewriting address information, often with port mappings so multiple private clients can share public IPv4 addresses.'),
 ('ipv4','IPv4','The widely used version of IP with 32-bit addresses, usually written as four decimal octets. Private and public addressing have different routing contexts.'),
 ('ipv6','IPv6','The version of IP with 128-bit addresses. A service can have IPv4 and IPv6 paths with different reachability.'),
 ('arp','ARP','Address Resolution Protocol: a mechanism for finding a local-link address for a directly reachable IPv4 destination, commonly the next-hop router.'),
 ('mtu','MTU','Maximum Transmission Unit: the largest network-layer packet size carried on a link without link-specific fragmentation. Tunnels and other encapsulation can reduce the usable size.'),
 ('tcp','TCP','Transmission Control Protocol: a transport that supplies an ordered, reliable byte stream while a connection is usable. It does not guarantee application completion.'),
 ('udp','UDP','User Datagram Protocol: a transport for datagrams without built-in guarantees of delivery or order. Higher-level protocols can add reliability over it.'),
 ('quic','QUIC','A secure transport over UDP that supplies streams and congestion control. HTTP/3 uses QUIC instead of TCP.'),
 ('handshake','Handshake','An exchange used to establish connection state or security parameters. TCP and TLS have different handshakes.'),
 ('flow-control','Flow control','Mechanisms preventing a sender from overwhelming the receiver’s available capacity. In TCP this is distinct from protecting shared network capacity.'),
 ('congestion-control','Congestion control','Mechanisms that adapt sending to shared network capacity. They are different from application quotas such as requests per minute.'),
 ('connection-pool','Connection pool','A finite set of reusable client connections. Pool waiting and connection establishment are different contributors to elapsed time.'),
 ('tls','TLS','Transport Layer Security: a protocol providing protected communication and authentication mechanisms. HTTPS normally validates the server’s identity through certificates.'),
 ('https','HTTPS','HTTP using a secure transport with server authentication. Successful HTTPS does not guarantee API authorization or a correct model answer.'),
 ('certificate','Certificate','A signed statement binding identity information to a public key under a trust system. Clients validate the chain, name, dates, and applicable rules.'),
 ('sni','SNI','Server Name Indication: TLS information identifying the hostname the client intends to contact, allowing servers to select appropriate configuration and certificates.'),
 ('mtls','mTLS','Mutual TLS: TLS with certificate-based client authentication as well as server authentication. It is not automatically used by ordinary API-key-based HTTPS.'),
 ('authentication','Authentication','Establishing an identity. TLS server identity and the client’s API identity are separate authentication questions.'),
 ('authorization','Authorization','Deciding what an identified caller is permitted to do. A reachable service can still reject an authenticated request.'),
 ('http','HTTP','Hypertext Transfer Protocol: application-level request/response semantics, including methods, fields, and status codes. HTTP versions differ in framing and transport.'),
 ('header','Header','A field carrying message metadata, such as Content-Type or Authorization. Some headers are sensitive and should be redacted from diagnostics.'),
 ('api','API','Application Programming Interface: a contract for interacting with software. A network API adds application rules above its underlying protocols.'),
 ('rpc','RPC','Remote Procedure Call: a style of invoking an operation on another process or machine. Failures can leave uncertainty about whether remote work happened.'),
 ('grpc','gRPC','An RPC framework commonly using HTTP/2 and Protocol Buffers. Its application status must be interpreted in addition to the HTTP exchange.'),
 ('cors','CORS','Cross-Origin Resource Sharing: browser-enforced rules governing a page’s access to responses from other origins. curl success does not establish browser permission.'),
 ('sse','SSE','Server-sent events: a text event-stream format commonly used for server-to-client streaming over HTTP. Event framing and application completion are separate concerns.'),
 ('websocket','WebSocket','A protocol for bidirectional messages over a persistent connection. It differs from SSE and from raw TCP read boundaries.'),
 ('ttft','Time to first token','Elapsed time until the client identifies the first generated content token or event under a stated measurement definition. It is distinct from first byte and completion latency.'),
 ('latency','Latency','Elapsed time for a defined operation. Always name the boundaries: connection setup, first token, completed response, or whole logical sample.'),
 ('rtt','RTT','Round-trip time: elapsed time for a network exchange to go to a destination and back. It is not the same as total model response time.'),
 ('bandwidth','Bandwidth','A capacity for carrying data, commonly measured in bits per second. It differs from achieved throughput and from latency.'),
 ('throughput','Throughput','The achieved rate of useful work, such as completed samples per second. Specify whether you count attempts, logical samples, bytes, or tokens.'),
 ('percentile','Percentile','A value locating an observation within a distribution under a chosen convention. Tail percentiles need enough observations and a clear failure-handling policy.'),
 ('concurrency','Concurrency','The number of operations active at the same time. It is not a rate per minute and does not by itself enforce a provider quota.'),
 ('rate-limit','Rate limit','A restriction on work over time, such as requests or tokens within a policy window. Limits can apply across many workers or clients.'),
 ('backpressure','Backpressure','Slowing or limiting upstream production when downstream capacity is constrained, so waiting and resource use stay bounded.'),
 ('timeout','Timeout','A limit on a particular wait. Library semantics vary; distinguish connection, pool, idle-read, and total-operation limits.'),
 ('deadline','Deadline','A time budget or cutoff for an operation. An end-to-end deadline must account for its included waits and retries.'),
 ('retry','Retry','Another attempt after an outcome that the policy deems retryable. It can duplicate work and should retain its own attempt identity.'),
 ('idempotency','Idempotency','The property that repeating an operation has the same intended effect as performing it once. It does not imply identical responses or zero additional cost.'),
 ('jitter','Jitter','In retry policies, randomized delay that reduces synchronized retries. In network performance discussions, it can instead mean variation in delay.'),
 ('proxy','Proxy','An intermediary handling traffic on behalf of a caller or service. It can affect destinations, security boundaries, buffering, timing, and error responses.'),
 ('load-balancer','Load balancer','A component distributing traffic across backends. Its decision unit may be a connection or an application request, depending on its layer and implementation.'),
 ('vpc','VPC','Virtual Private Cloud: AWS terminology for a configurable virtual network. It contains addressing and connectivity configuration, not automatic trust or encryption.'),
 ('egress','Egress','Traffic leaving an environment. An eval worker’s outbound model request is egress from the worker network.'),
 ('ingress','Ingress','Traffic entering an environment. Kubernetes also uses Ingress as the name of an API object describing incoming HTTP routing.'),
 ('security-group','Security group','In AWS, a stateful allow control associated with resources. It is separate from route tables, network ACLs, and application authorization.'),
 ('network-namespace','Network namespace','An isolated view of networking resources such as interfaces, routes, and sockets. Containers can have separate namespaces or deliberately share one.'),
 ('container','Container','An isolated process environment using operating-system facilities. Its networking depends on namespace and runtime configuration, not merely on its image.'),
 ('pod','Pod','A Kubernetes unit whose containers share networking and some other resources. localhost can connect containers in the same Pod, not arbitrary Pods.'),
 ('service','Kubernetes Service','An abstraction for accessing a set of backends. A typical ClusterIP Service provides stable cluster-internal access, but other Service types and headless Services exist.'),
 ('endpointslice','EndpointSlice','A Kubernetes object describing network endpoints associated with a Service. Checking it helps distinguish a Service name from usable backend targets.'),
 ('networkpolicy','NetworkPolicy','A Kubernetes object constraining selected traffic flows when enforced by a supporting network plugin. Its presence alone does not prove enforcement.'),
 ('region','Region','A provider-defined geographical deployment area. Different regions can imply different routes, latency, availability assumptions, and transfer costs.'),
 ('availability-zone','Availability zone','A provider-defined isolated deployment location within a region. Specific failure and placement guarantees depend on the cloud provider.'),
 ('nccl','NCCL','NVIDIA’s library for collective communication among GPUs. Its internal traffic is distinct from the external HTTPS path to a model endpoint.'),
 ('trace','Trace','Related observations representing a request’s path through operations. A distributed trace depends on instrumentation and propagated context.'),
 ('span','Span','A timed operation within a trace, with identifiers and attributes that help relate it to other operations.'),
 ('request-id','Request ID','An identifier supplied or propagated by a component for correlating a request with logs. Different systems may use different IDs for the same logical sample.'),
 ('attempt','Attempt','One execution of a request or operation. Several attempts may belong to one logical evaluation sample.'),
 ('circuit-breaker','Circuit breaker','A policy that temporarily stops calls to a failing dependency and controls recovery probes. It is not a substitute for recording failed or unattempted work.'),
 ('sandbox','Sandbox','An environment that constrains code or tool execution. Its filesystem, identity, and network permissions are part of an agent evaluation’s task conditions.'),
]
root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
glossary={slug:dict(name=name,short=definition.split('. ')[0]+('.' if not definition.split('. ')[0].endswith('.') else ''),defn=definition) for slug,name,definition in ENTRIES}
for entry in glossary.values(): entry['def']=entry.pop('defn')
(root/'research/glossary.json').write_text(json.dumps(glossary,indent=2))
(root/'assets/glossary-data.js').write_text('window.GLOSSARY = '+json.dumps(glossary,indent=2)+';\n')
print(f'Built {len(glossary)} glossary entries.')
